-- ScholarMesh -- 08b -- seed notes: Machine Learning + Linear Algebra
INSERT INTO DOCUMENT (title, description, file_path, file_type, file_size_bytes, visibility, content, student_id, course_id, upload_date)
SELECT v.title, v.description, v.file_path, 'md', octet_length(v.content), v.visibility, v.content,
       s.student_id, c.course_id, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP - v.age
FROM (VALUES
  ('Linear Regression and Gradient Descent'::varchar, 'Hypothesis, squared-error cost, the gradient descent update rule, learning rate effects, batch vs stochastic updates and the normal equation.'::varchar, 'seed/linear-regression-gd.md'::varchar, 'public'::varchar, 'priya.nair@scholarmesh.test'::varchar, 'UE23CS352A'::varchar, INTERVAL '40 days', $n$Linear regression predicts a number from input features using a straight line (a flat plane when there are many features). The hypothesis is h(x) = w0 + w1*x1 + ... + wn*xn. Example: predicting rent from area. If w0 = 3000 and w1 = 20, a 500 sq ft flat gets 3000 + 20*500 = 13000 rupees.

We need a score for how wrong the weights are. The standard one is mean squared error: J(w) = (1/2m) * sum of (h(x_i) - y_i)^2, over m training examples. Squaring makes errors positive and punishes big misses more; the 1/2 just cancels when differentiating. For linear regression J is bowl-shaped (convex), so there is one global minimum and no local traps.

Gradient descent walks downhill on that bowl. Every step updates all weights together: w_j := w_j - alpha * (1/m) * sum of (h(x_i) - y_i) * x_ij. Alpha is the learning rate. Too small and training crawls; too large and the steps overshoot the minimum, so the cost bounces around or even grows. Plot J against iterations: it should fall smoothly. Scaling features to similar ranges speeds things up.

Batch gradient descent uses all m examples per step: accurate but slow on big data. Stochastic gradient descent (SGD) updates after one example, so steps are cheap but noisy. Mini-batch is the usual compromise. The alternative is the normal equation, w = (X^T X)^-1 X^T y, which gives the weights in one shot with no learning rate. But it gets expensive with many features (inverting is roughly cubic) and fails if X^T X is not invertible.$n$::text),
  ('Bias-Variance Trade-off and Regularization', 'Underfitting vs overfitting, bias and variance, validation curves, L1 vs L2 regularization and choosing lambda by cross-validation.', 'seed/bias-variance-regularization.md', 'public', 'meera.pillai@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS352A', INTERVAL '35 days', $n$Underfitting means the model is too simple to capture the pattern, for example fitting a straight line to data that curves. It does badly on both training data and new data. Overfitting means the model is so flexible that it memorises noise in the training set, for example a degree-15 polynomial through 20 points. It looks perfect on training data but does badly on new data.

These map to bias and variance. Bias is error from wrong assumptions: a high-bias model is too rigid and misses the true pattern. Variance is how much the learned model changes if we train on a different sample: a high-variance model chases noise. Expected test error is roughly bias squared plus variance plus irreducible noise, so making a model more complex lowers bias but raises variance.

Learning curves show this. Plot error against model complexity: training error keeps falling, while validation error falls and then rises again. A big gap between the two (low training error, high validation error) signals overfitting. Both being high and close together signals underfitting.

Regularization fights overfitting by adding a penalty on weight size to the loss. Ridge (L2) adds lambda * sum of w_j^2, which shrinks all weights smoothly toward zero. Lasso (L1) adds lambda * sum of |w_j|, and its sharp corner pushes many weights to exactly zero, so it also does feature selection (sparsity). Lambda controls the strength: too large and the model underfits, too small and it overfits. We choose it by k-fold cross-validation: try several values and pick the one with the lowest average validation error.$n$),
  ('Decision Trees and Random Forests', 'How trees split using entropy and Gini, how to stop overfitting, and why bagging with random feature subsets cuts variance.', 'seed/decision-trees-random-forests.md', 'public', 'ishita.das@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS352A', INTERVAL '27 days', $n$A decision tree classifies by asking a series of yes/no questions about features, like 'is petal length < 2.5 cm?'. Each internal node is a test, each branch an answer, and each leaf a prediction. Training picks, at every node, the split that makes the child nodes as pure as possible, meaning each contains mostly one class.

Purity is measured by entropy, H = -sum p_k log2 p_k, or by Gini impurity, G = 1 - sum p_k^2. Information gain is the parent's entropy minus the weighted average entropy of the children, and we choose the split with the highest gain. Example: a node with 5 yes and 5 no has entropy 1 bit, while a node with 10 yes and 0 no has entropy 0. Gini behaves similarly but avoids the logarithm.

If we keep splitting, the tree grows until every leaf is pure and memorises the training data, which is overfitting (high variance). We control this with max depth, a minimum number of samples per leaf, or by pruning, which cuts back branches that do not improve validation accuracy.

A random forest fixes the instability of a single tree using bagging: draw many bootstrap samples (random samples with replacement) of the data and train one deep tree on each. It also uses random feature subsets: at each split only a random handful of features (about sqrt of the total) are considered, so the trees are less alike. Predictions are combined by majority vote or averaging. Averaging many weakly correlated trees cancels out their individual errors, so variance drops while bias stays about the same.$n$),
  ('Support Vector Machines and the Kernel Trick', 'Maximum-margin classification, support vectors, the soft margin parameter C and how kernels with gamma handle non-linear data.', 'seed/svm-kernel-trick.md', 'course', 'ananya.rao@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS352A', INTERVAL '22 days', $n$A support vector machine (SVM) separates two classes with a line (a hyperplane in higher dimensions). Many lines can split the data, so it picks the one with the maximum margin: the widest empty street between the classes. The decision rule is sign(w.x + b) and the margin width is 2/||w||, so a wider margin means a smaller ||w||.

Only the points closest to the street decide where it goes. These are the support vectors, lying exactly on the margin edges. Delete any other point and the boundary does not move. Real data is rarely perfectly separable, so the soft margin lets some points sit inside the margin or on the wrong side, at a price. The parameter C sets that price: large C punishes violations heavily (narrow margin, overfits), small C tolerates them (wider margin, smoother).

Some data is not linearly separable, like one class forming a ring around another in 2D. The fix is to map points into a higher-dimensional space where a flat hyperplane works: adding the feature x1^2 + x2^2 turns the ring into a clean split. The kernel trick avoids computing that map: the SVM only needs dot products between points, and a kernel K(x, z) gives the dot product in the high-dimensional space straight from the original vectors.

Common kernels: polynomial, K = (x.z + 1)^d, and RBF (Gaussian), K = exp(-gamma * ||x - z||^2), which corresponds to infinitely many dimensions. In RBF, gamma sets how far one point's influence reaches. High gamma gives tight, wiggly boundaries around single points (overfit); low gamma gives smooth, almost linear ones. In practice C and gamma are tuned together with cross-validation.$n$),
  ('Precision, Recall and ROC Curves', 'Confusion matrix, accuracy paradox, precision, recall, F1, thresholds, ROC and AUC, and when precision-recall curves are better.', 'seed/precision-recall-roc.md', 'public', 'aditya.joshi@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS352A', INTERVAL '15 days', $n$Accuracy alone can be misleading. Say 1 in 100 transactions is fraud, and a lazy model labels everything 'not fraud'. It is 99% accurate yet catches zero fraud. This is the accuracy paradox on imbalanced data. The confusion matrix gives the full picture: true positives (TP), false positives (FP), true negatives (TN) and false negatives (FN).

Precision = TP / (TP + FP): of everything the model flagged, how much was right? Recall = TP / (TP + FN): of all the real positives, how many did we find? Example: 40 real frauds, the model flags 50 transactions and 30 are truly fraud. Precision = 30/50 = 0.6 and recall = 30/40 = 0.75. F1 is the harmonic mean, 2PR / (P + R) = 0.667.

Most classifiers output a score or probability, and a threshold turns it into a label. Raising the threshold makes the model pickier: precision usually goes up and recall goes down. Lowering it does the reverse. The right trade-off depends on the cost: a cancer screening test wants high recall, while a spam filter wants high precision so real mail is not lost.

The ROC curve plots true positive rate (recall) against false positive rate, FP / (FP + TN), as the threshold sweeps from high to low. A random guesser lies on the diagonal. AUC, the area under the curve, is the probability that a random positive is ranked above a random negative: 0.5 is chance, 1.0 is perfect. But with heavy imbalance, FPR stays tiny because TN is huge, so ROC looks rosy. A precision-recall curve is more honest there since it ignores TN.$n$),
  ('K-Means Clustering Step by Step', 'The assignment and update loop of k-means, k-means++ start, choosing k and where the algorithm struggles.', 'seed/kmeans-clustering.md', 'private', 'kavya.menon@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS352A', INTERVAL '6 days', $n$K-means groups unlabelled data into k clusters, where k is chosen by us. Each cluster is represented by its centroid, the mean of its points. The algorithm repeats two steps. Assignment: give every point to the nearest centroid, usually by Euclidean distance. Update: move each centroid to the mean of the points assigned to it.

Tiny example: 1D points 1, 2, 9, 10 with k = 2 and starting centroids 1 and 2. Assignment: point 1 joins the first centroid, and 2, 9, 10 join the second. Update: the centroids become 1 and 7. Reassign: point 2 is now closer to the first centroid (distance 1 against 5). Update again: 1.5 and 9.5, and nothing changes after that, so it has converged.

Neither step can increase the total within-cluster squared distance, so k-means always converges. But it may reach a local minimum that depends on the starting centroids; a bad start can split one natural cluster and merge two others. k-means++ initialisation helps: pick the first centroid at random, then choose each next one with probability proportional to its squared distance from the nearest chosen centroid.

To choose k, the elbow method plots the within-cluster sum of squares against k and looks for the bend where adding clusters stops helping much. The silhouette score compares a point's distance to its own cluster with the nearest other cluster, from -1 to 1; higher is better. Limitations: it assumes roughly round, similar-sized clusters, so crescents defeat it. It uses distances, so features must be scaled first, or a feature in thousands will dominate one in single digits.$n$),
  ('Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors, Intuitively', 'What Av = lambda v means geometrically, the characteristic equation with a 2x2 example, diagonalisation and applications.', 'seed/eigen-intuition.md', 'public', 'priya.nair@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23MA241B', INTERVAL '32 days', $n$Most vectors change direction when a matrix A multiplies them. Eigenvectors are the special ones that do not: Av = lambda v. The matrix only stretches or shrinks them (or flips them if lambda is negative) along the same line, and lambda, the eigenvalue, is the stretch factor.

To find them, rewrite Av = lambda v as (A - lambda I)v = 0. A non-zero v exists only if A - lambda I is singular, so det(A - lambda I) = 0, the characteristic equation. Example: A = [[2,1],[1,2]]. Then det(A - lambda I) = (2-lambda)^2 - 1 = (lambda-1)(lambda-3) = 0, so lambda = 1 or 3. For lambda = 3, solving (A - 3I)v = 0 gives v = (1,1); for lambda = 1 it gives v = (1,-1).

A matrix with n independent eigenvectors can be diagonalised: A = P D P^-1, where the columns of P are the eigenvectors and D is diagonal with the eigenvalues. In the eigenvector basis A just scales each axis, which makes powers easy: A^k = P D^k P^-1. For the example, A^10 only needs 1^10 and 3^10. Symmetric matrices like this one always have real eigenvalues and perpendicular eigenvectors.

Uses: Google's PageRank is the eigenvector (eigenvalue 1) of the web's link-probability matrix, giving each page its importance score. In dynamical systems x_next = A x, the system is stable if every eigenvalue has magnitude below 1, and it blows up if any is above 1. PCA and vibration modes also come from eigen-decomposition.$n$),
  ('SVD and PCA for Dimensionality Reduction', 'Singular value decomposition, low-rank approximation and how PCA falls out of it, including centring and explained variance.', 'seed/svd-pca.md', 'public', 'kavya.menon@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23MA241B', INTERVAL '18 days', $n$The singular value decomposition (SVD) factorises any matrix as A = U Sigma V^T. U and V are orthogonal (their columns are the left and right singular vectors) and Sigma is diagonal with singular values s1 >= s2 >= ... >= 0. Geometrically, A rotates (V^T), stretches along the axes (Sigma), then rotates again (U). Unlike eigendecomposition, it works for any shape of matrix.

Singular values measure importance: s_i says how much the matrix stretches space along the i-th direction. Writing A as a sum of rank-1 pieces, A = s1 u1 v1^T + s2 u2 v2^T + ..., and keeping only the first k terms gives the best rank-k approximation. Example: keeping k = 50 terms of a 1000 x 1000 image needs about 50 x 2001 = 100,000 numbers instead of 1,000,000.

PCA (principal component analysis) uses this for dimensionality reduction. First centre the data by subtracting each feature's mean, otherwise the first component tends to point toward the mean. The principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix C = X^T X / (n-1), ordered by eigenvalue. The first is the direction of greatest variance, the second is the best direction perpendicular to it, and so on.

The link: if X is the centred data matrix and X = U Sigma V^T, then X^T X = V Sigma^2 V^T. So the columns of V are the principal components, and the variance along component i is s_i^2 / (n-1). The explained variance ratio of component i is its share of the total variance; we keep enough components to reach, say, 95%, then project onto them.$n$),
  ('Vector Spaces, Rank and Null Space', 'Span, independence, basis, dimension, column space, null space and the rank-nullity theorem with a worked example.', 'seed/vector-spaces-rank.md', 'private', 'arjun.mehta@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23MA241B', INTERVAL '10 days', $n$A vector space is a set of vectors you can add and scale without ever leaving the set, like R^2 or R^3. The span of some vectors is everything you can build from them as linear combinations c1 v1 + c2 v2 + .... Vectors are linearly independent if the only way to combine them into the zero vector is with all coefficients zero, so none is redundant.

A basis is a set of vectors that is independent and spans the whole space. Every vector then has exactly one way of being written in terms of the basis. The dimension is the number of vectors in any basis, so R^3 has dimension 3. Example: (1,0), (0,1) and (1,1) span R^2 but are dependent, because (1,1) = (1,0) + (0,1); drop one and you get a basis.

For an m x n matrix A, the column space C(A) is the span of its columns, i.e. all vectors b for which Ax = b has a solution. The null space N(A) is the set of all x with Ax = 0. The rank is the dimension of the column space, which equals the number of pivots after row reduction.

Rank-nullity theorem: rank(A) + nullity(A) = n, the number of columns. Example: A = [[1,2,3],[2,4,6]]. The second row is twice the first, so row reduction leaves one pivot and the rank is 1. Then nullity = 3 - 1 = 2. Indeed Ax = 0 reduces to x1 + 2x2 + 3x3 = 0, with x2 and x3 free, so the null space is spanned by (-2,1,0) and (-3,0,1).$n$)
) AS v(title, description, file_path, visibility, owner_email, course_code, age, content)
JOIN STUDENT s     ON s.email = v.owner_email
LEFT JOIN COURSE c ON c.course_code = v.course_code;
